WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.740
Here at It’s Lit it could be misconstrued
that we are just Ivory Tower Literary Elites

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:12.089
with great hair, but the truth is … we are
that, but we’re nerds too.

00:00:12.089 --> 00:00:17.610
In the past few decades literature has expanded
to not only mean the “novel”, but “graphic

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.160
novels” as well.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.380
Today we are gonna break down how the graphic
novel went from the comic book store to the

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.380
classroom.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.310
Narrative art that tells stories has existed
in some form since before civilization.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:34.230
From cave paintings, to Egyptian hieroglyphics,
to English serialized engravings.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.570
And then we got COMICS!!

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:45.570
But the first big precursor to the graphic
novel is thought to be Histoire de M. Vieux

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.740
Bois, drawn by Swiss artist and writer Rodolphe
Töpffer in 1828.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:57.140
Topffer used sequential imagery accompanied
by text to tell the story of the titular Monsieur

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.290
Vieux Bois’s comic misadventures.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:04.650
By 1841, unauthorized English translations
of Topffer’s work had made their way to

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.880
the United States under the title “The Adventures
of Obadiah Old Buck”.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:14.080
Topffer, in the note to Mr Jabot wrote:
“This little book is mixed in nature.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.670
It is made up of a series of pictures [...] each
one of which is accompanied by a line or two

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:20.670
of text.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:25.210
The pictures without the words would only
have a very obscure meaning, while the text

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.930
without the pictures signifies nothing at
all.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.530
The whole taken together is a kind of novel.”

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.530
….a…

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.530
GRAPHIC one

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.180
While mostly forgotten to modern audiences
now, comics theorist Scott McCloud has called

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:42.150
Topffer the “father of the modern comic,”
"cartooning and panel borders" along with

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:47.119
"the first interdependent combination of words
and pictures seen in Europe"

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:51.549
Broadly speaking, a graphic novel is a collection
of related comic strips that form a story

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:53.869
and are all published together as one book.

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:58.350
Back in the day what really separated comics
from graphic novels was the higher quality

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.350
format of the book itself.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.340
Better paper and being bound rather than stapled.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:08.800
Surrealist painter German Max Ernst is credited
with creating the proto-graphic novel.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.340
In what was called the “Collage novel”
Ernst would take images from other publications

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.320
and link them together with a narrative.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.841
American Cartoonist, Milt Gross, published
the pantomime tale He Done Her Wrong: The

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:28.810
Great American Novel and Not a Word in It
— No Music, Too, in 1930 a “wordless novel”

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.980
that many consider another precursor to the
graphic novel.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:38.700
While not super popular it was impactful in
the industry and was a huge influence on Osamu

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.950
Tezuka aka “the God of Manga” so like
super niche.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.870
These “wordless novels” were part of the
German Expressionist movement of the early

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.530
20th century that were killed off in its nation
of origin following the rise of Nazi censorship

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:53.590
in Germany.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:59.060
However, it’s influence already gave birth
to the artistic idea that would become the

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.060
“graphic novel.”

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.720
Now, you might be asking, “Princess, why
not use the word “comic” or “comic book”

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.140
instead of “graphic novel?

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.349
It sounds like the same thing to me.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:14.080
And--well--you’re not necessarily wrong;
the term “graphic novel” is a fairly nebulous

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.830
one that artists, writers, critics, and fans
have debated over the last few decades, but

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:19.830
we’ll get into that momentarily.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:26.120
The term was originally coined in 1964 comic
fanzine called Capa-Alpha, by Richard Kyle

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.400
but it didn’t take off as a popularized
term until Will Eisner wrote A Contract with

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.750
God and Other Tenement Stories.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.599
Sara J. Van Ness it was shared that:

00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:39.220
When he first proposed the book to a New York
publisher, Eisner recalls, “A little voice

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:44.280
inside me said, ‘Hey stupid, don’t tell
him it’s a comic or he’ll hang up on you.’

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.180
So I said, “It’s a graphic novel.”

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.970
Eisner’s intuitive hesitation in labeling
his work as a comic book suggests that he

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:57.370
was conscious of some negative implications
that the comic book label carried.”

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:01.420
This collection of four stand-alone stories
was something Eisner intended for “adults”

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.849
and wanted to be published in bookstores.

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:08.680
On the trade paperback of the title it said
“graphic novel.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.180
Making it the “first” of that mainstream
label.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:17.600
Contract was not the first graphic novel;
many cite Jack Katz’s The First Kingdom,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:23.909
which came out a few years before Contract,
but what it did was elevate the term “graphic

00:04:23.909 --> 00:04:29.240
novel” and comics as a long-form but contained
storytelling device into the mainstream.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:34.520
This was because Eisner worked to get it stocked
in actual bookstores, validating it’s literary

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.469
value in the eyes of adult readers.

00:04:37.469 --> 00:04:42.611
Following Contract we had titles like Sabre
by Don McGregor and artist Paul Gulacy, which

00:04:42.611 --> 00:04:49.629
takes place in the year 2020 where “Earth
has succumbed to global famine, energy crises,

00:04:49.629 --> 00:04:54.009
a plague based on a leaked American-government
bioweapon” … huh…

00:04:54.009 --> 00:04:59.999
But this is when we began to see the “prestige”
division between “super serious” graphic

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:01.789
novels and comics.

00:05:01.789 --> 00:05:07.590
Titles like Batman: Year One, Sandman, and
The ‘Nam all serve to elevate the way readers

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:08.819
saw the medium.

00:05:08.819 --> 00:05:13.720
Sandman is especially notable because as Joe
Sutliff Sanders, a specialist in children's

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:19.399
media at the University of Cambridge, explains,
“Sandman did not create the market for ongoing

00:05:19.399 --> 00:05:25.910
graphic-novel series, did not even exactly
drive it, but it did demonstrate to readers,

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:31.830
vendors, distributors, publishers, and writers
that the future of comics lay not in monthly

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.900
serials but in bound collections.”

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:40.099
Look, as a nerd who loves comics, I don’t
view graphic novels as inherently “superior,”

00:05:40.099 --> 00:05:47.639
but anyone whose read Fables or Saga can tell
you how a good long comic book run can nourish

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:48.639
the soul.

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:52.990
Yes, it is cool when one artist team creates
a powerful stand alone story, but just because

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:55.069
it’s long doesn’t mean it’s good.

00:05:55.069 --> 00:05:57.310
Which is just solid advice in general.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:03.559
The major moment for graphic novels came in
1992 when I was born—and when Art Spiegelman’s

00:06:03.559 --> 00:06:08.490
Maus became the first (and is still the only)
graphic novel to win a Pulitzer.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:14.219
Noted for using postmodernism expression by
representing Jews as mice and Germans as cats,

00:06:14.219 --> 00:06:19.220
the story depicts Spiegelman interviewing
his father about his experiences as a Polish

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.729
Jew and Holocaust survivor.

00:06:21.729 --> 00:06:23.270
Professor of German and Jewish Studies.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.369
PhD, University of Virginia, Erin McGlothlin
explains that:

00:06:25.369 --> 00:06:30.580
“Within the field of Holocaust Studies,
Maus is considered a key literary text by

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:36.050
the scholarship on testimony and trauma [...] In
the cultural sphere of comics, on the other

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:41.710
hand, it is celebrated chiefly on account
of its format, narrative, and graphic innovation

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:46.529
and its inventive approach to subject matter
previously deemed unsuitable for the comics

00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:47.529
medium.”

00:06:47.529 --> 00:06:52.371
Maus is now taught in schools along with titles
like Persepolis by Marjane Satrapi, which

00:06:52.371 --> 00:06:57.840
depicts her childhood up to her early adult
years in Iran during and after the Islamic

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:58.869
Revolution.

00:06:58.869 --> 00:07:02.900
Persepolis has been subsequently praised as
a postcolonial text.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:07.670
As Matthew P. McAllister and Stephanie Orme,
wrote in “Cinema’s DIscovery of the Graphic

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:11.650
Novel: Mainstream and Independent Adaptation”
for Cambridge Press:

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:18.569
“Instead of depicting Iranian women as universally
oppressed and as in need of Western liberation,

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:24.440
Persepolis offers a more complete, nuanced
view of Iranian women’s lives--as actively

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.909
resisting what they consider “oppressive”
on their own terms.”

00:07:27.909 --> 00:07:33.210
Through this we began to see the memoir graphic
novel really emerge as a powerful storytelling

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.929
device for coming of age stories.

00:07:35.929 --> 00:07:40.589
Craig Thompson explores his upbringing in
an Evangelical Christian family and eventually

00:07:40.589 --> 00:07:42.689
leaving Christianity in Blankets.

00:07:42.689 --> 00:07:49.270
2006’s Fun Home: A Family Tragicomic by
lesbian cartoonist Alison Bechdel, details

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.739
her childhood struggles with gender identity
and childhood abuse.

00:07:52.739 --> 00:07:59.089
It was later turned into a musical that ended
up taking home the Tony Award for Best Musical.

00:07:59.089 --> 00:08:04.699
Justin Hall explains: “Fun Home was an important
moment for queer graphic novels, both because

00:08:04.699 --> 00:08:09.279
of its reception by the mainstream literary,
academic, and cultural establishments and

00:08:09.279 --> 00:08:15.789
because of how it helped push the comics industry
to pay more attention to LGBTQ material.”

00:08:15.789 --> 00:08:20.270
These stories created the foundation for many
younger cartoonists and illustrators to tell

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.239
stories using this longer comic book form.

00:08:22.239 --> 00:08:27.849
Especially now, with independent comic publications
outside of the big two (DC/Marvel), places

00:08:27.849 --> 00:08:32.159
like First Second Books and others have really
allowed stories to shine.

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.899
Queer creators like Noelle Stevenson known
for Lumberjanes and Nimona and Tillie Walden

00:08:35.899 --> 00:08:42.039
(Spinning) have used the format to tell engaging
stories that highlight that comics are not

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:46.379
and have never solely been a format for cis-white
hetero dudes.

00:08:46.379 --> 00:08:52.160
Jen Wang, writer of Stargazing and The Prince
and the Dressmaker and works by Raina Telgemeier

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.490
have entered schools as well, which gives
students new avenues to explore literature

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.279
made for them.

00:08:58.279 --> 00:09:03.730
Reducing graphic novels and comics in general
to an inferior artform because it’s primary

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:08.069
audience isn’t always adults is just baseless
snobbery.

00:09:08.069 --> 00:09:12.740
Storytelling doesn’t belong to any one format
and what makes a book literary isn’t just

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:16.750
that there are words on a page—it is what
stories are being told and how it speaks to

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:18.420
the human experience.

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:23.810
For some of us it’s Grant Morrison’s All-Star
Superman instead of …(checks high brow list)

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.250
Infinite Jest.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:30.420
So for certain authors who think Graphic Novels
aren’t books … well you are wrong.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:33.829
Kathryn Strong Hansen writes “In Defense
of Graphic Novels”:

00:09:33.829 --> 00:09:38.529
“Many teachers have shown how graphic novels
can help energize students whose interests

00:09:38.529 --> 00:09:43.829
are hard to capture, can aid low-level and
nonnative English-speaking readers through

00:09:43.829 --> 00:09:49.279
the twinning of words with images, and can
challenge higher-level readers to expand their

00:09:49.279 --> 00:09:53.399
analytical skills to include consideration
of visual elements.”

00:09:53.399 --> 00:09:58.430
The only people who are still dismissing Graphic
Novels are frankly out of touch and maybe

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:05.050
the issue is your own limited idea of what
literature is, not that Superman is in it.

